602739726,"title":"President of Niger","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Daouda Malam Wanké"},{"qid":"Q1032","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q356835","value_label":"Salou Djibo","from_t":2010.131506849315,"to_t":2011.2630136986302,"title":"President of Niger","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Salou Djibo"},{"qid":"Q1032","kind":"flag","value_qid":"Q103046","value_label":"Flag of Niger.svg","from_t":1959.8931506849315,"to_t":null,"title":"flag of Niger","property":"P163","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"flag of Niger"}],"people":{"Q120965176":{"label":"Abdourahamane Tiani","description":"Nigerien army general and commander of the Nigerien presidential guard","enwiki":"Abdourahamane Tiani","summary":"Abdourahamane Tiani (born 1 January 1964) is a Nigerien military officer and politician who has served as the 11th president of Niger since 2025. Known for his anti-imperialist, pan-Africanist and Hausa nationalist views, Tiani served as the chief of the Nigerien presidential guard from 2011 to 2023 when he overthrew president Mohamed Bazoum in a coup, becoming the leader of a junta. He was inaugurated as president of Niger two years later.\nBorn in Toukounous to a Hausa family, Tiani received military training from the National School of Active Officers in Thiès, Senegal.","image":"Abdourahamane Tchiani in 2025 (cropped).jpg","birth":1961,"death":null},"Q2646711":{"label":"Ali Lamine Zeine","description":"Nigerien politician","enwiki":"Ali Lamine Zeine","summary":"Ali Mahamane Lamine Zeine (born 1965) is a Nigerien politician and economist who has served as the prime minister of Niger since 2023. He previously served as minister of the Economy and Finance from 2003 to 2010.","image":"Ali Lamine Zeine, IMF 2008.jpg","birth":1965,"death":null},"Q365417":{"label":"Ibrahim Baré Maïnassara","description":"Nigerien President (1949-1999)","enwiki":"Ibrahim Baré Maïnassara","summary":"General Ibrahim Baré Maïnassara (9 May 1949 – 9 April 1999) was a Nigerien military officer and diplomat who ruled Niger from 1996 until his assassination in 1999. He seized and lost power in military coups.\nBaré Maïnassara, a Maouri, a subgroup of Niger's Hausa ethnic majority, was born in Dogondoutchi in 1949 and pursued a military career. Maïnassara was named Army Chief of Staff in March 1995, under a constitution that had moved Niger from prolonged military rule in 1991.","image":"Ibrahim Bare Mainassar illustration.png","birth":1949.3506849315067,"death":1999.268493150685},"Q57482":{"label":"Mahamadou Issoufou","description":"President of Niger from 2011 to 2021","enwiki":"Mahamadou Issoufou","summary":"Mahamadou Issoufou (born 1 January 1952) is a Nigerien politician who served as the president of Niger from 7 April 2011 to 2 April 2021. Issoufou was the prime minister of Niger from 1993 to 1994, president of the National Assembly from 1995 to 1996, and a candidate in each presidential election from 1993 to 2016. He led the Nigerien Party for Democracy and Socialism (PNDS-Tarayya), a social democratic party, from its foundation in 1990 until his election as president in 2011. During the presidency of Mamadou Tandja (1999–2010), Issoufou was the main opposition leader.","image":"Mahamadou Issoufou-IMG 3645.jpg","birth":1952,"death":null},"Q220227":{"label":"Mamadou Tandja","description":"President of Niger from 1999 to 2010","enwiki":"Mamadou Tandja","summary":"Mamadou Tandja (1938 – 24 November 2020) was a Nigerien politician who was President of Niger from 1999 to 2010. He was President of the National Movement for the Development of Society (MNSD) from 1991 to 1999 and unsuccessfully ran as the MNSD's presidential candidate in 1993 and 1996 before being elected to his first term in 1999. While serving as President of Niger, he was also Chairman of the Economic Community of West African States from 2005 to 2007.\nTandja was reported to be the son of a Peul (Fulani) father from Mauritania and a Kanuri mother.","image":"Tandja in Nigeria June 2007.jpg","birth":1938.5479452054794,"death":2020.896174863388},"Q314355":{"label":"Diori Hamani","description":"President of Niger from 1960 to 1974 (1916-1989)","enwiki":"Hamani Diori","summary":"Hamani Diori (6 June 1916 – 23 April 1989) was the first President of the Republic of Niger. He was appointed to that office in 1960, when Niger gained independence from France. Although corruption was a common feature of his administration, he gained international respect for his role as a spokesman for African affairs and as a popular arbitrator in conflicts. His rule ended with a military coup in 1974.","image":"Hamani Diori 1968b.jpg","birth":1916.4289617486338,"death":1989.3068493150686},"Q319430":{"label":"Mahamane Ousmane","description":"President of Niger from 1993 to 1996","enwiki":"Mahamane Ousmane","summary":"Mahamane Ousmane (born 20 January 1950) is a Nigerien politician. Elected as the fourth President of Niger at 43 years old, he is the youngest elected president in Africa. He was also the first democratically elected president of his country, serving from 16 April 1993 until he was deposed in a military coup d'état on 27 January 1996. He has continued to run for president in each election since his ouster, and he was president of the National Assembly from December 1999 to May 2009.","image":"Mahamane Ousmane.jpg","birth":1950.0520547945205,"death":null},"Q3318398":{"label":"Mohamed Bazoum","description":"President of Niger from 2021 to 2023","enwiki":"Mohamed Bazoum","summary":"Mohamed Bazoum (born 1 January 1960) is a Nigerien politician who served as president of Niger from 2021 to 2023. He assumed office in April 2021 after winning the 2020–21 presidential election and surviving a coup d'état attempt. He was ousted in the 2023 Nigerien coup d'état by members of the presidential guard and the armed forces led by Omar Tiani. He was arrested and subsequently charged with treason.","image":"Mohamed Bazoum at the European Commission - P060245-844068 (cropped).jpg","birth":1960,"death":null},"Q348886":{"label":"Daouda Malam Wanké","description":"President of Niger (1946-2004)","enwiki":"Daouda Malam Wanké","summary":"Daouda Malam Wanké (May 6, 1946 – September 15, 2004) was a military and political leader in Niger. He was a member of the Hausa ethnic group.\nWanké's year of birth is disputed. Many sources claim it is 1954 while others suggest 1946.","image":null,"birth":1946.3424657534247,"death":2004.704918032787},"Q356835":{"label":"Salou Djibo","description":"military leader of Niger from 2010 to 2011","enwiki":"Salou Djibo","summary":"Salou Djibo (born 15 April 1965) is a Nigerien Army officer. After President Mamadou Tandja's attempts to remain in power beyond his term, Djibo led the military coup of 18 February 2010 that ousted Tandja, after which he became the head of the Supreme Council for the Restoration of Democracy. The Supreme Council returned power to a new civil government after the 2011 elections.","image":"Général de corps d'armée Salou Djibo.jpg","birth":1965.2849315068493,"death":null}},"media":{"Flag of Niger.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Flag_of_Niger.svg/330px-Flag_of_Niger.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Niger.svg","author":"Made by: Philippe Verdy User:verdy_p, see also fr:Utilisateur:verdy_p.","license":"Public domain"},"Coat of arms of Niger.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Coat_of_arms_of_Niger.svg/330px-Coat_of_arms_of_Niger.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Niger.svg","author":"Pbroks13 (talk)","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},"Abdourahamane Tchiani in 2025 (cropped).jpg":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/Abdourahamane_Tchiani_in_2025_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abdourahamane_Tchiani_in_2025_(cropped).jpg","author":"Sule Barma","license":"Public domain"},"Ali Lamine Zeine, IMF 2008.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Ali_Lamine_Zeine%2C_IMF_2008.jpg/330px-Ali_Lamine_Zeine%2C_IMF_2008.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ali_Lamine_Zeine,_IMF_2008.jpg","author":"IMF Staff Photograph/Ryan Rayburn","license":"Public domain"},"Ibrahim Bare Mainassar illustration.png":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5d/Ibrahim_Bare_Mainassar_illustration.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ibrahim_Bare_Mainassar_illustration.png","author":"Voice of America","license":"Public domain"},"Mahamadou Issoufou-IMG 3645.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Mahamadou_Issoufou-IMG_3645.jpg/330px-Mahamadou_Issoufou-IMG_3645.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahamadou_Issoufou-IMG_3645.jpg","author":"Rama","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0 fr"},"Tandja in Nigeria June 2007.jpg":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e0/Tandja_in_Nigeria_June_2007.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tandja_in_Nigeria_June_2007.jpg","author":"Ibrahim Alfa Ahmed, Voice of America Hausa Service","license":"Public domain"},"Hamani Diori 1968b.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Hamani_Diori_1968b.jpg/330px-Hamani_Diori_1968b.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hamani_Diori_1968b.jpg","author":"Kroon, Ron / Anefo","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0 nl"},"Mahamane Ousmane.jpg":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/61/Mahamane_Ousmane.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahamane_Ousmane.jpg","author":"Niger77","license":"Public domain"},"Mohamed Bazoum at the European Commission - P060245-844068 (cropped).jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Mohamed_Bazoum_at_the_European_Commission_-_P060245-844068_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Mohamed_Bazoum_at_the_European_Commission_-_P060245-844068_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mohamed_Bazoum_at_the_European_Commission_-_P060245-844068_(cropped).jpg","author":"Photographer: Bogdan Hoyaux","license":"CC BY 4.0"},"Général de corps d'armée Salou Djibo.jpg":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/G%C3%A9n%C3%A9ral_de_corps_d%27arm%C3%A9e_Salou_Djibo.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G%C3%A9n%C3%A9ral_de_corps_d%27arm%C3%A9e_Salou_Djibo.jpg","author":"Annassara","license":"Public domain"}},"names":["Categoria:Níger","NE","NER","NIG","Niger","Nijer","Níger","Republic Niger","Republic of Niger","Republic of the Niger","Republiek Niger","Republik Niger","República del Níger","Res publica Nigritana","République du Niger","The Niger","la République du Niger","ne","Νίγηρας","Нигер","Республика Нигер","النيجر","جمهورية النيجر","زندر","عاصمة النيجر","قائمة جزر النيجر","منطقة حضارية نيامي","نایجر","نيجر","نیجر","नाइजर","ニジェール","ニジェール共和国","尼日","尼日尔","尼日爾"]}